    /// <summary>
    /// MLEDIT's crosses (CC, OC, MC) of a closed multiline against the records AutoCAD 2021 left (accoreconsole, read back
    /// through <c>DxfOut</c> of a <c>Wblock</c>), STANDARD at scale 20 justified at the top. Where no pair of segments can be
    /// made, AutoCAD takes a closed multiline as the run of its segments from the first to the last, as it takes an open one:
    /// its first segment is run on past its start and its last past its end, and no other, so a second that meets the square
    /// (0, 0)-(100, 0)-(100, 100)-(0, 100) past the corner (100, 0) on the line of its bottom edge, or past (0, 100) on the line
    /// of its left edge, is <c>These multilines do not intersect.</c> (a vertical x = 102 to 140 from y = -60 to 60), where the
    /// same past (0, 0) or any other corner is <c>Invalid operation on a self intersecting multiline.</c> (a horizontal
    /// y = -40 across x = 60 to 160). Each row is the option, the points of the first multiline ("c:" before them for a closed
    /// one), those of the second, where each is picked and what AutoCAD answered, as <see cref="MlineTeePairTests"/> writes
    /// them; rows whose picks lie within 30 of the other multiline's path are left out (AutoCAD may take the other
    /// multiline for the pick).
    /// </summary>
    public class MlineClosedCrossPastCornerTests
